hi there this is a bonus video it's not  one of my regularly scheduled videos  it's not really about language learning  it's about the importance of disagreeing  the importance of entertaining different  points of view and recognizing that  people don't always have to agree and  the reason I'm doing this is because I'm  a little bit peeved I should say having  just had a look at the video that the  interview that I did with with Matt Matt  versus Japan on his channel and I  referenced it in the discussion we had  in Japanese I think there was a link in  the description box at any rate I'll  make sure that there's a link here where  we spoke for about thirty minutes most  of it in English most of it was Matt  explaining how he went about learning  Japanese and his approach to learning  Japanese and what has prompted me to  make this video is is as I review their  comments I'm just absolutely taken aback  by the amount of sort of venom and  hostility that is expressed by the vast  majority of people commenting on this  video and there are a lot of them and  the gist of it is that I am close-minded  I don't want to you know accept Matt's  way of learning Japanese probably I  can't speak Japanese anyway and you know  you can go there and have a look but I  looked at this as a kind of holy cow but  it's part of a larger phenomenon and  this is the sort of phenomenon that that  somehow we are not allowed to disagree I  have to be blown away with whatever Matt  is doing I have to consider that so  wonderful when in fact I don't think  that I think that he  is perfectly entitled to pursue you know  the enth degree of perfection in  Japanese which is a perfectly valid  thing to pursue and I said that I  thought it was certainly that's what his  choices that's what he chooses to do but  I have a different approach and my  approach is that I like to learn a lot  less in less depth about a number of  languages but even that in the comments  is considered to be you know very  amateurish and dilettante ish and so  forth and so on but you know I've also  made the point in our original  discussion that that whatever he wants  to pursue in Japanese greater depth of  knowledge and some aspect of Japanese  all of this comes through more  engagement with the language more  listening you want to know more about  business Japanese you listen to and read  more business Japanese you want to do  more on history you do more reading and  listening on history you will never  cover at all but as long as you're  driven by an interest in those things of  course you should do that and there's no  way I don't respect that that's a  perfectly valid thing to do  however in my case I have listened to  audio books on history and on literature  and have read books in certainly 18  languages so I've listened to history  books in Polish and Czech and in  Ukrainian and in Romanian even though I  don't speak the language very well and  not to mention German and Swedish and  French and Chinese and Japanese and  podcasts in Korea and so that's what I  like to do and that gives me a broader  range of experience through the language  a broader range of knowledge and I'm not  saying that what I do is superior but it  is it is a choice but the suggestion in  all these comments and maybe I'm  overreacting is that you know I have to  be blown away with what Matt is doing I  have to think that's tremendous I have  to go wow and that's obviously a  superior approach and it's just a choice  but but it is part of a broader picture  I was once at a conference here in  Vancouver  they talked about you know what was it  called dialoguing so you're not supposed  to disagree any longer you're just  supposed to kind of massage around and  come to the same kind of conclusion I  mean that's what's been a big part of  this whole political correctness thing  there is one correct way of doing things  and everybody must somehow coalesce  around this and I'm saying that in  history if Galileo didn't say that in  fact the Earth revolves around the Sun  if no one questioned church dogma if we  don't have lively debates about subjects  we're never going to get past the  established conventional wisdom and  therefore we should encourage people to  debate challenge what they're saying you  know by all means get in there and say I  don't like that because of this reason  and that reason but what we see more of  is this sort of you know why don't you  agree you know I find that even on I've  had the odd exchange on my channel here  where I ask people for their opinions  and someone comes forward with an  opinion and then I say well no actually  I don't agree with that I still feel the  way I originally felt and then the  person says well if he didn't want my  opinion why did you ask for it well I  didn't necessarily say that I was going  to agree with everything that people  said I'm just interested in hearing  other opinions because when we hear  other opinions and when we're involved  in a debate or a discussion we very very  rarely persuade other people that we are  right but we listen and as we listen we  learn things at the very least we're  forced to reassess our views and to  justify those views to ourselves and to  the other person whom we may not  persuade but at least we have to think  it through and it's also possible that  some of the things that the other person  says may influence how we feel but all  of that only happens if we can actually  have a debate and debates suggest that  we disagree and so I'm saying that  because I I just want to get this off my  chest I looked at those comments I'll  leave the link to Matt versus whatever  pants Steve whatever on his channel I  let him speak for 15 to 20 minutes or  hardly said anything and then I  commented on what I thought of his  approach and for whatever reason that  has earned the wrath of the vast  majority of his followers and I just  think it's unhealthy further to be that  degree of ill-will towards an opinion  that diverges from whatever is the sort  of conventional dogma amongst the  followers of the met and I say that  without wanting to take anything away  from Matt who is actually a very  pleasant person to talk to and who  speaks excellent Japanese and whom I  respect tremendously for what he has  done with his Japanese particularly  given the fact that he has only lived in  Japan for six months so he has learned  his Japanese essentially in the United  States and by the way I only say you  don't need to be in the country to learn  the language and being in the country  doesn't guarantee that you will learn  the language so in that sense Matt is  also proof of that so I just want to get  that off my chest it's out there and  probably will trigger a whole bunch more  negative comments but I think  particularly in this day and age when  there's so much sort of arrogance and  intolerance in how people present their  views we need to take a step back like  everything I say here now if somebody  wants to come along and produce a video  that totally disagrees with me that's  fine too but then I may come back and  disagree with that person in turn and  that's what's called open discussion  free discussion debate ideas challenging  each other and all of that can be done  without casting aspersions on the  person's character so thank you for  listening and there you have it  bye for now
 

